title: SPIR-V Specification
author: John Kessenich, Google
author: Boaz Ouriel, Intel
author: Raun Krisch, Intel
changes:

=== Changes from Version 1.5, Revision 1

- Khronos Issue #511: Allow non-execution non-memory scopes in the introduction to the
  <<Scope_-id-, Scope _<id>_ section >>.
- Khronos MR !147: Fix <<OpFNegate, *OpFNegate*>> so it handles 0.0f properly
- Khronos Issue #502: <<OpAccessChain, *OpAccessChain*>> array indexes must be an in-bounds for logical pointer types.
- Khronos Issue #518: Include both *VariablePointers* and *VariablePointersStorageBuffer* capabilities in the
  <<Validation, validation>> rules when discussing variable pointer rules.
- Khronos Issue #496: Allow *Invariant* to <<Decoration, decorate>> a block member.
- Khronos Issue #469: Disallow *OpConstantNull* result and *OpPtrEqual*, *OpPtrNotEqual*, and *OpPtrDiff* operands
  from being pointers into the *PhysicalStorageBuffer* storage class.
  See the <<PhysicalValidation, *PhysicalStorageBuffer* validation rules>>.
- Khronos Issue #425: Clarify what variables can allocate pointers, in the <<Validation, validation rules>>,
  based on the declarations of the *VariablePointers* or *VariablePointersStorageBuffer* capabilities.
- Khronos Issue #442: Add a note pointing out where <<UnsignedSigned, signedness>> has some semantic meaning.
- Khronos Issue #498: Relaxed the set of allowed types for some <<Group, Group and Subgroup instructions>>.
- Khronos Issue #500: Deprecate <<OpLessOrGreater, *OpLessOrGreater*>> in favor of <<OpFOrdNotEqual, *OpFOrdNotEqual*>>.
- Khronos Issue #354: Rationalize <<Literal, literals>> throughout the specification.
  Remove "immediate" as a separate definition.
  Be more rigid about a single literal mapping to one or more operands,
  and that the instruction description defines the type of the literal.
- Khronos Issue #479: Disallow intermediate aggregate types that could not be used to declare global variables,
  and disallow all types that can't be used for declaring variables.
  See the <<ShaderValidation, shader validation>> "Type Rules".
  Also, more strongly state that intermediate values don't form a storage class, in the introduction to
  <<Storage_Class, storage classes>>.
- Khronos Issue #78: Use a more correct definition of <<BackEdge, _back edge_>>.
- Khronos Issue #492: Overflow with <<OpSDiv, **OpSDiv**>>, <<OpSRem, **OpSRem**>>, and <<OpSMod, **OpSMod**>>
  results in undefined behavior.
